.TH CCAPPLE 1 "2026-11-01" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccapple \- read\-only search of the Apple apps' local stores on a Mac
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccapple status
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ccapple contacts
.RI [ \-\-name
.IR text ]
.RI [ \-\-phone
.IR digits ]
.RI [ \-\-email
.IR text ]
.RI [ \-\-org
.IR text ]
.br
.B ccapple messages
.RI [ \-\-person
.IR name\-or\-handle ]
.RI [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-until
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.br
.B ccapple calendar
.RI [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-until
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-calendar
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.br
.B ccapple reminders
.RI [ \-\-list
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.RI [ \-\-open\-only ]
.br
.B ccapple notes
.RI [ \-\-title
.IR text ]
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.RI [ \-\-folder
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-until
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-id
.IR n ]
.RI [ \-\-include\-deleted ]
.RI [ \-\-full ]
.br
.B ccapple photos
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.RI [ \-\-since
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-until
.IR date ]
.RI [ \-\-near
.IR lat,lon[,km] ]
.RI [ \-\-album
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-uuid
.IR uuid ]
.RI [ \-\-include\-deleted ]
.br
.B ccapple home
.RB ( \-\-homes " | " \-\-rooms " | " \-\-zones " | " \-\-accessories " | " \-\-scenes " | " \-\-automations )
.RI [ \-\-home
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-room
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-text
.IR words ]
.PP
Every search subcommand also takes
.RI [ \-\-limit
.IR n ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-db
.IR path ].
.SH DESCRIPTION
The data a person keeps in the Apple apps \(em the address book, the calendar,
reminders, notes, messages, the photo library \(em is reachable only through the
GUI apps or through frameworks that ask the user for permission app by app. A
Claude Code session can drive neither. So a session asked "when did the supplier
last write to me?" or "which photo has the serial number on it?" has to say it
cannot look.
.PP
.B ccapple
closes that gap without a new grant, account or dependency. The apps keep their
data in ordinary SQLite files that iCloud already syncs onto the Mac, and a
terminal that has been given Full Disk Access can open them. The tool opens each
one
.B read\-only
and prints the few rows a filter selects. It is standard\-library Python and has
no network access.
.PP
Six of the subcommands each read one app: contacts, messages, calendar,
reminders, notes and photos.
.B home
reads the layout of the Apple Home (HomeKit) configuration.
.B status
reads none of their content: it reports, per store, where the file is,
whether it
opens, and row counts.
.SH THE SAFETY FLOOR
These stores hold other people's messages and the operator's private material,
so the limits below are properties of the code, not conventions.
.TP
.B Read\-only, by construction
Every store is opened through one function, with the SQLite URI
.IR "file:<path>?mode=ro&immutable=1" ,
followed by
.IR "PRAGMA query_only=ON" .
.B mode=ro
refuses writes. The
.B immutable=1
option takes no lock and never touches the store's
.I \-wal
and
.I \-shm
files, so the app that owns the store is never disturbed. Nothing here writes to
a store, copies one, sends a message, or drives a GUI app (no AppleScript, no
.IR open ).
.TP
.B Search, never dump
Every search subcommand refuses to run without at least one filter (exit
status 2), and every search is capped by
.B \-\-limit
(default 20). Whole\-store extraction is not offered.
.TP
.B Locked notes stay locked
A password\-protected note's body is encrypted. The tool never reads it and
never tries to decrypt it. Such a note is listed, flagged
.IR locked ,
with no text, and a
.B \-\-text
search cannot see inside one (it says how many it skipped).
.TP
.B Credentials are never selected
The Home store holds credential\-shaped columns beside the ones the tool keeps
(passwords, pre\-shared keys, pairing keys, Matter credentials, a Wi\-Fi
name). Every
query names the columns it wants and none of those is among them; there is no
.IR "SELECT *" ,
so a column a future macOS adds is not exposed by accident. A test fails if any
of the known credential columns appears in the Home code, and another plants
sentinel values in them and checks that no output carries one.
.TP
.B A stale view, said plainly
Because
.B immutable=1
does not read the write\-ahead log, the newest rows (the last few messages, an
edit made seconds ago) can be missing, and in principle a page can be torn
if the
app is mid\-write. That is acceptable for search; re\-check a value that
matters.
For Home, which is checkpointed only now and then, the tool warns on standard
error when a non\-empty log sits beside the store.
.PP
What a search prints goes wherever the caller's output goes \(em into the
session
transcript, for a Claude Code session. Filter narrowly, and keep the output out
of any repository, document or message shared with other people.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS status
Per store: its path, whether it opens read\-only, and row counts only \(em never
content. The first thing to run on a new machine: a store that cannot be opened
is reported as
.I CANNOT OPEN
with the reason (usually that the terminal lacks Full Disk Access), and the exit
status is 1 if any store cannot be.
.SS contacts
Finds people by
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-phone ,
.B \-\-email
or
.BR \-\-org .
Every address\-book source is searched. Names match case\- and
accent\-insensitively (\(lqmaori\(rq finds \(lqMāori\(rq) and all words
must be present. A phone number matches on its digits, so spacing and
punctuation do not matter. Records with no
name or organisation are never listed.
.SS messages
Searches iMessage and SMS by
.B \-\-person
(a contact name, resolved through Contacts, or a phone number or email used as
given),
.B \-\-since
/
.BR \-\-until ,
and
.B \-\-text
(words in the body). A person's one\-to\-one chat is returned, plus that
person's
own messages in group chats; other people's group messages are not. A name that
matches no contact with a phone number or email is a usage error, never a silent
\(lqno messages\(rq. Phone numbers match on their last nine digits, so
\fB+44 20 7946 0101\fR
and \fB020 7946 0101\fR agree.
.PP
Modern Messages keeps most bodies in a binary archive rather than the plain text
column. The tool decodes that archive on a best\-effort basis: it ignores
formatting and links, and where the shape is not what it expects it reports the
body as not decoded rather than guess. Because of this,
.B \-\-text
is matched in the tool, newest messages first, not in the database.
.SS calendar
Searches events by
.BR \-\-since / \-\-until ,
.B \-\-calendar
(name contains) and
.B \-\-text
(title, notes or location). Times are shown in local time; all\-day events
as plain
dates. Recurring events are
.B not
expanded by this tool: inside a date range they appear only for the occurrences
Calendar itself has cached (a rolling window), and with no date range they
appear
once, as the series, flagged
.IR repeats .
.SS reminders
Searches by
.B \-\-list
(name contains),
.B \-\-text
and
.B \-\-open\-only
(skip completed ones). Open reminders come first, soonest due first, undated
last.
Reminders marked for deletion are skipped.
.SS notes
Searches Apple Notes by
.B \-\-title
(contains),
.B \-\-text
(in the body),
.B \-\-folder
(path contains, e.g.\& \fIWork/Projects\fR),
.B \-\-since
/
.B \-\-until
(date modified) or
.B \-\-id
(a number from an earlier search). The default output is a snippet around the
match, not the note. Notes in Recently Deleted are left out unless
.B \-\-include\-deleted
is given.
.PP
.B \-\-full
prints one note's whole text, and only when the search matches exactly one note
(otherwise exit status 2: narrow the search or use
.BR \-\-id ).
Attachments appear in the text as
.IR [attachment] .
Note bodies are stored as a compressed protobuf message; the tool reads it
with a
small built\-in decoder, caps the decompressed size, and reports a body it
cannot
decode as such.
.SS photos
Searches the Photos library in place \(em nothing is copied \(em and returns
.B paths, not images
(converting HEIC for viewing is the caller's job, for example with
.BR sips (1)).
.B \-\-text
matches text Photos has recognised inside images and captions the user typed;
every word must match, as a substring. The other filters are
.BR \-\-since / \-\-until
(date taken),
.B \-\-near
.I LAT,LON[,KM]
(within KM kilometres, default 1; a leading minus on a southern latitude is
fine),
.B \-\-album
(name contains) and
.B \-\-uuid
(an asset from an earlier search). Trashed assets are left out unless
.B \-\-include\-deleted
is given.
.PP
Each result says whether the original file is on this Mac (with optimised
storage
most are only in iCloud) and gives the best viewable file: the original if
present,
otherwise the largest cached image derivative, otherwise none.
.SS home
Reads the Apple Home layout: one of
.BR \-\-homes ,
.BR \-\-rooms ,
.BR \-\-zones ,
.BR \-\-accessories ,
.B \-\-scenes
or
.B \-\-automations
is required (exactly one), and
.BR \-\-home ,
.B \-\-room
and
.B \-\-text
narrow it. It never controls anything: it cannot switch a light or run a
scene, and
the store holds no live state, so it cannot say whether a light is on.
Scenes are
listed by name and type only; what a scene does is not in the store. Accessories
show make, model, serial number, room, bridge and service types.
.PP
The output is a map of a home's devices. Keep it for the operator's own use.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIn\fR
Maximum results (default 20, at least 1). Applies to every search.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output: a JSON array of result objects (for
.BR status ,
an array of per\-store reports). Never styled.
.TP
.B \-\-db \fIpath\fR
Read this store file instead of the default location. Repeatable for
.B contacts
and
.BR reminders ,
which span several files. For
.B photos
it is the
.I Photos.sqlite
inside the
.I .photoslibrary
package; the search index and image files are found beside it.
.TP
.B \-\-contacts\-db \fIpath\fR
(messages) Contacts store to resolve names against. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-since \fIdate\fR, \-\-until \fIdate\fR
Dates are
.I YYYY\-MM\-DD
(or
.IR YYYYMMDD ,
as ccrepo and cctranscript spell them) in local time.
.B \-\-until
includes that whole day.
.TP
.B \-\-name \fItext\fR, \-\-phone \fIdigits\fR, \-\-email \fItext\fR, \-\-org \fItext\fR
(contacts) Filters. All given must match.
.TP
.B \-\-person \fIname-or-handle\fR, \-\-text \fIwords\fR
(messages) See above.
.TP
.B \-\-calendar \fIname\fR, \-\-list \fIname\fR, \-\-folder \fIname\fR, \-\-album \fIname\fR, \-\-title \fItext\fR
Name filters: case\- and accent\-insensitive \(lqcontains\(rq.
.TP
.B \-\-open\-only
(reminders) Skip completed reminders.
.TP
.B \-\-id \fIn\fR
(notes) One note, by the id an earlier search printed.
.TP
.B \-\-full
(notes) The whole text of the one matching note.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-deleted
(notes, photos) Also search Recently Deleted.
.TP
.B \-\-uuid \fIuuid\fR, \-\-near \fILAT,LON[,KM]\fR
(photos) See above.
.TP
.B \-\-homes, \-\-rooms, \-\-zones, \-\-accessories, \-\-scenes, \-\-automations
(home) Which kind of thing to list; exactly one.
.TP
.B \-\-home \fIname\fR, \-\-room \fIname\fR
(home) Narrow by home or room name.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the one\-screen digest (also available per subcommand).
.SH FILES
Default store locations, all under the home directory of the user running the
tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
~/Library/Application Support/AddressBook/Sources/*/AddressBook\-v22.abcddb
~/Library/Messages/chat.db
~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.calendar/Calendar.sqlitedb
~/Library/Group
Containers/group.com.apple.reminders/Container_v1/Stores/Data\-*.sqlite
~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.notes/NoteStore.sqlite
~/Pictures/Photos Library.photoslibrary/database/Photos.sqlite
~/Library/HomeKit/core.sqlite
.fi
.RE
.PP
These are Apple's private layouts. They were observed on macOS 26 and can change
between releases; where the tool reads an entity number or a join table it finds
it by name rather than assuming, but a larger change will make a subcommand fail
with a store error (exit status 1) rather than return a wrong answer.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check, once, that every store is reachable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple status
.fi
.RE
.PP
A phone number for a person:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple contacts \-\-name 'alex example'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The last messages with someone that mention an invoice:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple messages \-\-person 'alex example' \-\-text invoice \-\-limit 5
.fi
.RE
.PP
What is on the calendar next week, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple calendar \-\-since 2026\-11\-09 \-\-until 2026\-11\-15 \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open reminders on one list:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple reminders \-\-list errands \-\-open\-only
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find a note, then read the one you want in full:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple notes \-\-text 'spare batteries'
ccapple notes \-\-id 1234 \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.PP
Photos taken within 2 km of a point in a date range, then convert one to
JPEG so it
can be viewed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple photos \-\-near \-33.87,151.21,2 \-\-since 2026\-01\-01
sips \-s format jpeg "<viewable path>" \-\-out /tmp/photo.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
The accessories in one room:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccapple home \-\-accessories \-\-room kitchen
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including \(lqno matches\(rq).
.TP
.B 1
A store could not be read: missing, locked by macOS privacy (no Full Disk
Access),
or not the layout the tool expects. For
.BR status ,
at least one store cannot be opened.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: no filter given, a bad date or coordinate, an unknown person, a
.B \-\-full
that did not match exactly one note, or a
.B home
call without exactly one kind.
.SH NOTES
.B ccapple
is a capability instrument under
.IR docs/decisions/0006\-instruments\-in\-atelier.md :
its value is wholly the working relationship with Claude. The operator can
open the
Apple apps themselves; it exists so that the teammate can look up what the
operator is already able to see, with the narrowest reach that works. It is the
layer's first Python instrument, and needs only a Python 3.8 or later on
.B PATH
(it has been exercised on 3.9 and 3.14).
.PP
Full Disk Access is granted to the application that runs the command (the
terminal, or the editor hosting it), in System Settings under Privacy &
Security.
The tool cannot grant it, and does not try to.
.PP
Sending a message, creating an event or reminder, editing a note and
controlling a
home are deliberately absent. Each would need the operator's explicit approval
first, and a different design.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ccmail (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR sips (1)
